        <briefdescription>
<para>Evaluate plain-text fidelity via normalised edit distance. </para>
        </briefdescription>
        <detaileddescription>
<para><parameterlist kind="param"><parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>original</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The original text. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
<parameteritem>
<parameternamelist>
<parametername>recovered</parametername>
</parameternamelist>
<parameterdescription>
<para>The recovered text. </para>
</parameterdescription>
</parameteritem>
</parameterlist>
<simplesect kind="return"><para>RS in <computeroutput>[0.0, 1.0]</computeroutput>. </para>
</simplesect>
<simplesect kind="note"><para><bold>Large-string approximation:</bold> for inputs exceeding 10 000 characters the underlying <computeroutput>editDistance()</computeroutput> switches from the exact O(n×m) Levenshtein DP to an O(n) Hamming-style approximation (aligned-prefix character differences + length delta). This keeps RS computation under ~5 ms for 100 KB inputs at the cost of reduced accuracy when the edit distance is large relative to the string length. For document payloads beyond this size, consider using <computeroutput><ref refid="classthemis_1_1rag_1_1delegate__eval_1_1_xml_process_evaluator" kindref="compound">XmlProcessEvaluator</ref></computeroutput> or <computeroutput><ref refid="classthemis_1_1rag_1_1delegate__eval_1_1_json_document_evaluator" kindref="compound">JsonDocumentEvaluator</ref></computeroutput> instead. </para>
</simplesect>
</para>
